RULE R-47/T ("transport is a JSON value"): every type exported from `packages/contracts` must be a pure JSON value type — no class instance, no `Date`, no nominal/branded primitive, no `Map`/`Set`/`undefined`/`BigInt` — and `packages/contracts` must not depend on `@easytree/domain`. Conversion happens at exactly two named seams (see mappingSites). This is not a style rule; it is a consequence of what the domain types actually are: `TimeInterval` is a class with `#private` fields and a `private constructor` (packages/domain/src/time-interval.ts:107-134), reachable only via `TimeInterval.create` (time-interval.ts:144), and the file header states outright that there is deliberately no revival-from-JSON path (time-interval.ts:68-71). Branded ids are `string & {[brand]: "…"}` (identifiers.ts:14-17) — erased on the wire, and only re-obtainable through `unsafeIdentifier`, whose doc names "validiertes DTO" as a legitimate call site (identifiers.ts:57-67, esp. :61).

ENFORCEMENT — four independent, fail-closed legs. None is documentation.

(1) PACKAGE GRAPH (strongest, costs nothing). `packages/contracts/package.json` simply does not list `@easytree/domain`. This repo runs pnpm with isolated `node_modules` (no `.npmrc`; verified: `node_modules/@easytree` does not exist at the root, `packages/config/node_modules/@easytree` does not exist, and `apps/api/node_modules/@easytree/{config,domain}` exist only because apps/api declares them). An undeclared import therefore does not resolve — `pnpm typecheck` fails before any test runs, for value AND type-only imports. This is the same mechanism `packages/domain/src/index.ts:5-10` already relies on, and `packages/domain/package.json` proves it works: line 25 (`devDependencies`) is the only `*ependencies` key in the whole file — no `dependencies`, no `peerDependencies`.

(2) ARCHITECTURE GUARD — new rule `contracts-transport-only` in apps/api/test/architecture/rules.ts. Yes, the guard *can* express it, and the shape is exactly like the existing rules:
```ts
{
  id: "contracts-transport-only",
  inScope: (file) => file.startsWith("packages/contracts/src/"),
  check: (ref) => {
    if (ref.specifier === "@easytree/domain" || ref.resolved?.startsWith("packages/domain/") === true) {
      return `packages/contracts darf "${ref.specifier}" nicht importieren — Transporttypen sind JSON-Werte; TimeInterval ist eine Klasse und OrgId ein Brand, beide ueberleben JSON.parse nicht (EYT-47 AK 5).`;
    }
    return null;
  },
}
```
IS IT RIGHT? Yes, and the decisive argument is mechanical, not aesthetic. The guard is *specifier*-granular: `ImportRef` (scan.ts:35-45) carries `specifier`/`resolved`/`line`/`isTypeReference` and nothing else, and `ts.preProcessFile` (scan.ts:179) does not distinguish `import type` from `import`. So the guard can only express all-or-nothing on `@easytree/domain`. A softer rule ("type-only imports allowed, but not in DTO field position") is not expressible there at all and would have to be a type-level check. Given that, all-or-nothing is the only enforceable form — and it is also the correct one:
  a. A DTO typed with a domain type is a type that lies about the wire. `interval: TimeInterval` would mean `JSON.stringify` silently emits `{startUtc,endUtc}` (time-interval.ts:183-188) while the declared type claims a frozen class instance; the parsed value can never be assigned back to that type.
  b. `employeeId: EmployeeId` cannot come out of `JSON.parse` without a cast, and no OpenAPI/JSON-Schema generator can emit a brand or a class — it emits `string`. So the hand-written contract and the generated client would be two different types. That is literally the "manuelle DTO-Duplizierung" EYT-47 AC 3 forbids, reintroduced by the very import meant to avoid it.
  c. `packages/contracts` is consumed by `apps/web`. Letting domain in puts `TimeInterval` in the browser bundle *behind the DTO type*, which is where "just pass the entity through" starts.

DOES CONTRACTS LEGITIMATELY NEED DOMAIN FOR MAPPING? No — because the mapping layer does not live in contracts (see mappingSites). But there IS one real pull, and it must be answered honestly rather than waved away: two stable code vocabularies are explicitly declared to be part of the API contract inside domain/api code — `TIME_INTERVAL_ERRORS` ("Stabile Codes — sie werden Teil des API-Vertrags (EYT-47)", time-interval.ts:74-88) and `CONFLICT_CODES` ("Der Code ist Teil des API-Vertrags (EYT-47)", assignment-draft.ts:26-43). These are string-literal unions and would cross the wire fine. The direction cannot be reversed: `domain-allowlist` (rules.ts:91-117) forbids `packages/domain` from importing anything except relative paths inside domain and `@easytree/domain`, so domain can never import contracts. Resolution: contracts re-declares the vocabularies as its own `as const` arrays (they are wire vocabulary, not domain logic), and a **drift test in `apps/api/test/`** — the one place allowed to import both — asserts set-equality at runtime plus type-level mutual assignability:
```ts
type Exact<A, B> = [A] extends [B] ? ([B] extends [A] ? true : false) : false;
const _conflictCodesMatch: Exact<ConflictCodeDto, ConflictCode> = true;
expect([...CONFLICT_CODE_DTOS].sort()).toEqual([...CONFLICT_CODES].sort());
```
Adding a code on one side without the other then fails `unit-tests`. Duplication becomes a checked identity instead of an unchecked copy.

WHICH EXISTING RULES ALREADY APPLY TO packages/contracts (checked against rules.ts, no new code):
  - `no-app-to-app` (rules.ts:158-182) — inScope is `startsWith("apps/") || startsWith("packages/")` → APPLIES. Forbids contracts importing `@easytree/api`/`@easytree/web` or reaching into `apps/**`.
  - `no-generic-shared-package` (rules.ts:183-202) — inScope is `() => true` → APPLIES. This is the rule that *licenses the name*: "contracts" is not in `GENERIC_PACKAGE_NAMES` (rules.ts:76-85), so it passes as a "stabiles, fachlich benanntes Paket" per ADR-001:77, and ADR-002 §5 (:93-106) names it explicitly.
  - `no-fixtures-in-production-code` (rules.ts:203-212) — inScope is "not under test/e2e/__tests__" → APPLIES to `packages/contracts/src/**`.
  - `domain-allowlist` (rules.ts:87-89, 91-117) does NOT apply: `isDomainFile` is `startsWith("packages/domain/src/") || layerOf(file)==="domain"`, and `layerOf` only resolves under `apps/api/src/modules/` (rules.ts:59-66). So today NOTHING stops contracts from importing domain — which is exactly why rule (2) has to be added.
  TRAP: architecture.test.ts:66-68 asserts every rule watches ≥1 file. `contracts-transport-only` would be scope-empty and RED until `packages/contracts/src/**` exists. The rule and the package must land in the same commit, plus a red case in architecture-red-case.test.ts (same pattern as :151-158).

(3) JSON-VALUE ASSERTION (runtime, in `packages/contracts` vitest — runs inside the existing `unit-tests` job). For every exported DTO a canonical fixture must satisfy:
```ts
function isJsonValue(v: unknown): boolean {
  if (v === null || typeof v === "string" || typeof v === "number" || typeof v === "boolean") return true;
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return v.every(isJsonValue);
  if (typeof v !== "object") return false;                       // undefined, function, bigint, symbol
  const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(v);
  if (proto !== Object.prototype && proto !== null) return false; // Date, Map, Set, TimeInterval
  return Object.values(v as Record<string, unknown>).every(isJsonValue);
}
expect(isJsonValue(fixture)).toBe(true);
expect(JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(fixture))).toEqual(fixture);    // round-trip identity
```
A `TimeInterval` fails the prototype check (its prototype is `TimeInterval.prototype`, and it is `Object.freeze`d in the constructor, time-interval.ts:133); a `Date` fails it too, which is what enforces "times are strings, not Dates". The round-trip line is the direct executable form of AC 5.

(4) SCHEMA-DERIVED TYPES (makes the violation unwritable). DTO types are `z.infer<typeof …Schema>`, and the OpenAPI document is emitted from the same schemas. `zod@4.4.3` is already in the lockfile (packages/config/package.json) and ships `z.toJSONSchema` (verified at runtime in `packages/config/node_modules/zod`) — so generation is offline, no new dependency, no network beyond `pnpm install`. `z.infer` of a Zod schema is always a plain JSON-shaped type: a class or a brand cannot be produced, so a violating DTO cannot be authored in the first place.

CI: no new job. Spec generation, spec diff vs the committed `packages/contracts/openapi/v1.json`, client diff vs the committed client, the breaking-change check vs a committed `v1.baseline.json`, and the JSON-value assertions all run as vitest suites in `packages/contracts` and `apps/api` → `pnpm test` → `turbo run test` → existing required job `unit-tests` (.github/workflows/ci.yml:36-43). The sprint plan proposes a job named `contract-check` (docs/plans/2026-07-26-eyt-sprint-3-integrierter-slice.md:275) — I recommend deviating, and the plan's own convention supports it: line 185 requires every new CI job to be shipped together with `scripts/setup-branch-protection.sh` and the runbook, the script drift-checks `ci.yml` in BOTH directions and aborts (setup-branch-protection.sh:98-124, REQUIRED_CHECKS at :31-40), and re-applying ruleset 19718704 needs admin rights. This is the same trade EYT-46 already made: `architecture.test.ts` runs inside `unit-tests` (architecture.test.ts:5 "Läuft im Pflichtjob `unit-tests`"). Folding in costs nothing in blocking power — `unit-tests` is already required — and removes the ruleset-reapply step from the critical path. I could not find any evidence that a separate job is *required*; ADR-001:123 asks only that "OpenAPI-Diff und generierter Client laufen in CI", which `unit-tests` satisfies.

