MATCHES WHAT IS ALREADY EMITTED. `apps/api/src/common/http-exception.filter.ts` is `@Catch()` (no argument, :42) so it handles EVERY error, and always writes `ProblemDocument` (:15-21) via `res.status(status).json(body)` (:60). The contract records exactly that, with two additive extension members and nothing else changed:

```ts
// packages/contracts/src/error.dto.ts
export interface ProblemDocumentDto {
  /** ALWAYS the literal "about:blank" today — the filter hardcodes it
   *  (http-exception.filter.ts:53). Recorded as-is; see "known deviation" below. */
  readonly type: string;
  /** node:http STATUS_CODES[status] ?? "Error" (http-exception.filter.ts:54, :1).
   *  Human-readable, NOT machine-readable — never string-match on it. */
  readonly title: string;
  readonly status: number;
  /** For a deliberately thrown HttpException: its message, or `message[]` joined with "; "
   *  (detailOf, :31-40). For ANY unexpected error: the constant
   *  "An unexpected error occurred." (GENERIC_DETAIL, :23, :56) — internals and secrets are
   *  never surfaced. Proven by http-exception-filter.e2e.test.ts:39-54, which asserts the
   *  injected secret and stack frames are absent. */
  readonly detail: string;
  /** Echo of the request's x-correlation-id, or the literal "unknown" if the middleware did
   *  not run (:57). Same value the response carries in the x-correlation-id header
   *  (correlation-id.middleware.ts:7, :35). */
  readonly correlationId: string;

  // --- additive extension members, RFC-7807-legal, required by EYT-47 ---
  /** Stable machine-readable code. This is the field clients branch on — the reason
   *  CONFLICT_CODES and TIME_INTERVAL_ERRORS declare themselves part of the API contract
   *  (assignment-draft.ts:27, time-interval.ts:74). Absent for errors with no specific code. */
  readonly code?: ErrorCodeDto;
  /** Per-field / per-item detail for validation and conflict responses. Absent when empty —
   *  never `[]` vs `undefined` ambiguity. */
  readonly errors?: readonly ErrorItemDto[];
}

export interface ErrorItemDto {
  readonly code: ErrorCodeDto;
  /** JSON Pointer into the request body, e.g. "/interval/endUtc". Absent for whole-request
   *  errors. */
  readonly pointer?: string;
  /** Opaque, code-specific payload. Union-typed per code, never a free-form bag. */
  readonly stale?: PlanVersionStaleDto;
}
```

ERROR CODE SPACE (`ErrorCodeDto`) — assembled only from codes that a producer in this repo actually emits, following the discipline assignment-draft.ts:29-34 established when it deleted `RESOURCE_DOUBLE_BOOKED` for having no producer:
- the 6 `TIME_INTERVAL_ERRORS` verbatim (time-interval.ts:75-88): `START_MISSING`, `END_MISSING`, `START_INVALID`, `END_INVALID`, `START_EQUALS_END`, `END_BEFORE_START` — these are `TimeInterval.create`'s rejection reasons, i.e. the 400 body for a malformed interval;
- the 4 `CONFLICT_CODES` (assignment-draft.ts:36-41) for the 409 on publish;
- `TIME_ZONE_UNKNOWN` (planning-week.ts:57);
- transport-level codes introduced by this contract: `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_MISSING`, `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_INVALID`, `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`, `IDEMPOTENCY_IN_PROGRESS`, `PLAN_VERSION_STALE`, `PAGE_CURSOR_INVALID`, `PAGE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`, `RESULT_SET_TOO_LARGE`.
Same drift test as the conflict codes: `Exact<>` + set-equality in `apps/api/test/`, so adding a domain error code without adding it to the contract fails `unit-tests`.

TWO HONEST DEVIATIONS, both must be recorded rather than glossed:
1. CONTENT-TYPE IS `application/json`, NOT `application/problem+json`. `res.json()` (:60) sets `application/json`, and http-exception-filter.e2e.test.ts:44 asserts it. The OpenAPI document must therefore declare `application/json` for error responses. Switching to `problem+json` later is a breaking change and must go through the baseline bump.
2. `type` IS ALWAYS `"about:blank"`. Emitting a URN like `urn:easytree:error:plan-version-stale` for coded errors would be a genuine change to http-exception.filter.ts:53, not something the schema can assert about today's behaviour. RECOMMENDATION: leave `type` untouched and put all machine-readable meaning in `code`. That keeps the change purely additive — `toMatchObject` in the existing e2e tests (http-exception-filter.e2e.test.ts:42-48, :58-62) stays green — and it avoids two competing discriminators. Adding `code`/`errors` requires extending the filter (it currently constructs a closed `ProblemDocument`, :52-59); that is in scope for EYT-47 and should be its own commit with its own e2e assertion.

input.paginationSchema:
NOT SPECIFIED ANYWHERE IN THE REPO — DECISION TO BE RECORDED. Grep over `docs/prd/`, `docs/architecture/`, `docs/audit/`, `docs/handoff/` for pagination/paginier/cursor/seitenweise returns no product or architectural statement; the only hit anywhere is the sprint-3 plan restating the acceptance criterion (:277). Everything below is a proposal.

THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER IS TWO-PART, because the reads split cleanly into two kinds — and applying one scheme to both is where the generic blog pattern goes wrong here.

PART A — PLANNING READS ARE NOT PAGINATED. `getPlanningWindow` and `getMySchedule` (INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE.md:73-86) are bounded by the DOMAIN, not by data volume: a query is `(org, week)` or `(org, [from,to))`, and the pilot is "mindestens zwölf Nutzer und drei parallele Baustellen" (ADR-001:10). The four-week projection is already an explicit filter over a bounded key set — `projectWeeks(loads, weekKeys)` (weekly-capacity.ts:154-160).
Reasons NOT to paginate these, specific to this system rather than to page size:
1. The planner UI renders a WEEK GRID. A half-delivered week is not a smaller correct answer, it is a wrong picture.
2. INTEGRATION_ARCHITECTURE.md:110 sanctions client-side conflict logic as UX feedback. A client computing overlaps (`overlaps`, time-interval.ts:235-239) or weekly load (`aggregateWeeklyLoad`, weekly-capacity.ts:73-97) over page 1 of 3 will report "no conflict" for a genuine overlap sitting on page 2 — fail-open, and precisely the FIND-003 failure mode (a partial set treated as the whole set) re-created at the transport layer.
3. A cursor over a calendar window has no meaningful order to key on.
Instead: a hard server-side cap with an explicit, fail-closed error. Windows are capped at 4 ISO weeks (matching the rolling four-week planning the PRD assumes and `projectWeeks` implements); a result set beyond a configured maximum returns `422` with `code: "RESULT_SET_TOO_LARGE"` rather than a truncated array. Truncating silently is the one option that must not exist. The response echoes what it covers so the client can never mistake a window:
```ts
export interface PlanningWindowDto {
  readonly orgId: Uuid;
  readonly timeZone: IanaTimeZoneName;
  readonly weekKeys: readonly WeekKey[];   // exactly the weeks contained, in order
  readonly assignments: readonly AssignmentDraftDto[];   // complete for those weeks — no page
  readonly conflicts: readonly PlanningConflictDto[];
}
```

PART B — APPEND-ONLY HISTORIES USE KEYSET (CURSOR) PAGINATION. Plan-version history, audit entries and notifications are unbounded, immutable and strictly ordered (ADR-001:89 "Planrevisionen, Bestätigungen und Korrekturen bleiben historisch referenzierbar").
```ts
// request: query parameters
export interface PageQueryDto {
  /** 1..100, default 50. Bounded because the value reaches a SQL LIMIT. */
  readonly limit?: number;
  /** Opaque. Absent = first page. Never construct or parse client-side. */
  readonly cursor?: string;
}

// response envelope — generic, one shape for every paginated collection
export interface PageDto<T> {
  readonly items: readonly T[];
  /** null = end of collection. Explicit null, not absent: EYT-79 requires the terminal/empty
   *  state to be modelled, and an absent key is indistinguishable from a serialization bug. */
  readonly nextCursor: string | null;
}
```
WHY KEYSET AND NOT OFFSET, concretely: publishing appends at the HEAD of the version history (`revision` is monotone per (org, weekKey)). With `?offset=50`, a publish between page 1 and page 2 shifts every row by one and the client silently skips a version — in an audit trail. Keyset on `(published_at DESC, id DESC)` is stable under concurrent inserts because it names a position, not a count.
WHY NO `total`: it costs a second COUNT per page under RLS, it is stale the moment it is computed on a concurrently appended log, and computed carelessly (without the tenant predicate) it leaks the existence of rows the caller cannot read. Omitted until a UI actually needs it; adding it later is non-breaking, removing it would not be.
CURSOR CONTENT AND ITS SECURITY PROPERTY: base64url of `{orgId, publishedAt, id}` with an HMAC over the same. It MUST carry and verify `orgId`, because ADR-001:87 makes every such record tenant-bound and an unsigned cursor is otherwise a cross-tenant enumeration handle — a caller could paste another tenant's cursor and page through their audit log if server-side authorization were ever to lean on it. Verification failure or org mismatch -> `400` with `code: "PAGE_CURSOR_INVALID"`; never fall back to "start from the beginning", which is a fallback that turns a broken request into a plausible-looking success. `limit` above the maximum -> `400` `PAGE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`, not a silent clamp.

RECORD IN THE ADR: the max page size (100), the max planning window (4 weeks), the `RESULT_SET_TOO_LARGE` threshold, and the cursor signing key's rotation story. All four are currently unrecorded and are being proposed here, not read out of an existing document.

