PRIMARY BYPASS — edit the hand-written adapter the drift check never looks at.

`codegen.invocation` commits TWO files: `apps/api/src/platform/database/schema.generated.ts` (generated, byte-compared) and `apps/api/src/platform/database/kysely-schema.ts` (the hand-written mapped-type adapter producing the Kysely `DB` interface that every repository imports). `codegen.driftCheck` layer 1 compares only the first:

    tail -n +5 apps/api/src/platform/database/schema.generated.ts > /tmp/dbtypes-committed.ts
    diff -u /tmp/dbtypes-committed.ts /tmp/dbtypes-1.ts

So add this to the adapter and merge:

    // apps/api/src/platform/database/kysely-schema.ts
    type Generated_<T> = import("kysely").Generated<T>;
    export type DB = {
      [T in keyof Database["public"]["Tables"]]: /* generic mapping */ …
    } & {
      // "temporär gepinnt bis EYT-86" — org_id aus dem Insert-Typ entfernt
      items: Omit<Mapped<"items">, "org_id"> & { org_id?: string };
    };

Result across all nine required checks in ruleset 19718704:
- `format` — prettier-clean TypeScript → green (and the generated file is in .prettierignore anyway, per the design)
- `lint` — valid TS, eslint.config.mjs:6 ignores nothing relevant → green
- `typecheck` — valid TS → green
- `unit-tests` — layer 2 only hashes `supabase/migrations/*.sql` against the `// migrations-sha256:` header line inside `schema.generated.ts`; the adapter is not hashed → green
- `db-gates` — layer 1 regenerates and byte-compares `schema.generated.ts` only; the adapter is never regenerated and never compared → green
- build-web / web-smoke / build-api / secret-scan — untouched → green

The Kysely types and the migrations now disagree: a repository can compile an `items` insert with no `org_id`, and nothing fails until PostgreSQL rejects it at runtime (23502, or 42501 via items_insert_in_org, supabase/migrations/20260723222457_0002_tenancy.sql:193-195). The failure surfaces in production, not in CI. This is not hypothetical — `codegen.codegenTool` REQUIRES the adapter ("it is not a Kysely DB interface … the Generated<T> marker is derivable by a generic mapped type (one small hand-written file)"), and `tx.transactionPattern` rejects that whole approach for exactly this reason. The submitted design contains both halves.

SECONDARY BYPASS — untracked generated file.
`tx.transactionPattern` uses `git diff --exit-code -- apps/api/src/platform/database/database.types.ts`. MEASURED in this session (fresh repo, scratchpad): `git diff --exit-code -- <untracked path>` → exit 0; `git diff --exit-code -- <modified tracked path>` → exit 1. So if the generated file is never `git add`ed, or is later matched by a `.gitignore` glob (/Users/benjaminpoersch/EasyTree/.gitignore already carries bare globs such as `*.log`), the step regenerates it, diffs nothing, and reports green forever. Same outcome if the `--out-file` path and the `git diff --` path ever drift apart — e.g. someone changes `pnpm --filter @easytree/api exec` to a root-level invocation and the cwd-relative `src/platform/...` resolves elsewhere.

TERTIARY BYPASS — schema scope.
Both halves generate with `--schema public`. supabase/migrations/20260723222457_0002_tenancy.sql:10 already creates schema `app`. Any future migration adding a table outside `public` is invisible to generation AND to both drift layers, and both stay green.

MIGRATION-WITHOUT-REGENERATION, for completeness: this one IS caught, twice — layer 2 (unit-tests, ~1 s) because the migrations sha256 changes, and layer 1 (db-gates) because `supabase db reset` at ci.yml:104/108 rebuilds the DB the generator reads. That is the failure mode the design defends well; the three above are the ones it does not.

